<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippe Bunau-Varilla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philippe Bunau-Varilla was a French engineer who worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-de-lesseps">Ferdinand de Lesseps</a>'s failed Panama canal and later lobbied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to buy the French company's assets and build the canal in Panama rather than Nicaragua. After helping to organise Panama's secession from Colombia in November 1903, he acted as the new republic's envoy and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hay-bunau-varilla-treaty">Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty</a> before the Panamanian delegation reached Washington.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippe-bunau-varilla.jpg" alt="Philippe Bunau-Varilla" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippe_Bunau-Varilla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
